        "en" to GemDetails(
            "Seraphinite is a trade name for a variety of clinochlore, a chlorite-group mineral, a magnesium, iron and aluminium phyllosilicate. It formed through metamorphism and hydrothermal alteration of ultramafic rocks, when fluids transformed pyroxenes and olivines into chlorite. Its dark green mica fibres, grouped in feathers and fans, developed in veins where they could grow into rosettes; they give the angel-wing look behind its name, after the seraphim.",
            "Monoclinic system. Aggregates of flakes and fibres arranged in silvery feathers on a dark green ground. Perfect micaceous cleavage; flakes flexible but not elastic. Hardness 2 to 2.5, very soft; specific gravity 2.6 to 3.0; pearly to silky lustre; opaque; mean refractive index around 1.57 to 1.59. Cut as cabochons and carvings, it shows a silvery chatoyancy that moves with the light. Scratched by a fingernail, it polishes with difficulty and scratches quickly: reserved for pendants and brooches, often stabilised.",
            "Russia: the Lake Baikal region, Korshunovsk district (Irkutsk Oblast, Eastern Siberia), the only source of gem-quality seraphinite, in skarn iron deposits. Ordinary clinochlore occurs worldwide (Alps, Pennsylvania, Pakistan), but without the characteristic feathery pattern.",
            "A stone of regeneration and spiritual connection, said to soothe and help find a deep peace. It is associated with the heart and crown chakras and the sign of Sagittarius. Brief cleansing in lukewarm water without rubbing, or by smudging; recharging in moonlight or on a quartz cluster."
